## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design CKNE-aware scheduling for data pipelines, balancing fairness and performance under multi-tenant load.

## Key Concepts

- CKNE-to-slot mapping and data-freshness deadlines
- Minute-by-minute rebalancing with weighted fairness
- Baseline SLA guarantees and per-tenant minimums
- Telemetry: queue depths, latencies, CKNE correlations

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: compute per-tenant slot allocation from CKNE and queue depth
function allocateSlots(tenants, totalSlots, baseline) {
  const weights = tenants.map(t => t.ckne * A + (baseline - t.queue) / baseline * B);
  const sum = weights.reduce((x, y) => x + y, 0);
  return tenants.map((t, i) => Math.max(t.minSlots, Math.floor((weights[i] / sum) * totalSlots)));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt for tenant churn or abrupt CKNE shifts?
- What metrics would you expose to observe fairness and SLA adherence?

## Why This Is Asked
Prompts candidates to design CKNE-driven admission control and fairness in real-time multi-tenant streams, a practical production concern.

## Key Concepts
- CKNE signals to compute per-tenant health
- Dynamic rate limiting and cross-tenant shedding
- Payload schema for health metadata and token updates
- Minute-level control loop with smoothing

## Code Example

```javascript
function healthScore(p95Latency, errRate, queueDepth){
  const normLat = Math.min(p95Latency / 1000, 1);
  const normErr = Math.min(errRate, 1);
  const normQ = Math.min(queueDepth / 1000, 1);
  // Weighted mix: latency matters more
  return 0.55 * normLat + 0.25 * normErr + 0.20 * normQ;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test tail latency and fairness under correlated bursts?
- How would you handle tenants with bursty, short-lived traffic without starving others?

